ataset details**\n\n**Analyzing meter node count changes**\n\n**Planning canonical graph and projections strategy**\n**Designing multi-stage graph growth and retrieval**\n**Optimizing modular graph reachability metrics**\n\n**Planning schema relations and query cases**\n**Designing retrieval and pattern registry policies**\n**Analyzing component counts and degree distribution**\n\n**Inspecting M-type memories schema**\n\n**Planning targeted section review**\n\n**Designing typed projection strategy**\n**Outlining minimal query processing diagram**\n**Clarifying content match within topology constraints**\n\n**Planning hypergraph-native diffusion strategy**\n**Outlining phased implementation plan**\n\n**Summarizing content match validation strategy**\n**Refining graph pattern policy and roadmap**\n**Assessing per-pass metric limitations**\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI’ll read the topology note and inspect the nearby memory-system code/docs, then I’ll suggest a long-term graph strategy grounded in the current design and its invariants.\n\nI’ll read the topology note and inspect the nearby memory-system code/docs, then I’ll suggest a long-term graph strategy grounded in the current design and its invariants.\n\nThe key distinction is already visible: the current graph is mixing a storage/ontology structure with a retrieval index, and λ₂ is being asked to validate both. I’m checking the actual write, lifecycle, and recall paths now so the recommendation fits the machinery rather than just the metric.\n\nThe key distinction is already visible: the current graph is mixing a storage/ontology structure with a retrieval index, and λ₂ is being asked to validate both. I’m checking the actual write, lifecycle, and recall paths now so the recommendation fits the machinery rather than just the metric.\n\nA useful graph here should not be “as connected as possible.” The corpus is naturally modular, and many subjects are legitimate one-off context. The stronger target is query-relative: can a task seed reach the right reviewed memories, through semantically valid typed paths, within a bounded read—and does each edge type improve held-out retrieval? I’m turning that into a staged policy, including what to do about pattern minting.\n\nA useful graph here should not be “as connected as possible.” The corpus is naturally modular, and many subjects are legitimate one-off context. The stronger target is query-relative: can a task seed reach the right reviewed memories, through semantically valid typed paths, within a bounded read—and does each edge type improve held-out retrieval? I’m turning that into a staged policy, including what to do about pattern minting.\n\nThe long-term strategy should be: **one authoritative evidence hypergraph, several purpose-built retrieval projections, and no attempt to make the authoritative graph globally “well connected.”**\n\nA good memory graph is not one with a large λ₂. It is one in which a query can reach useful, reviewed memories through meaningful typed paths within a bounded budget—and where those paths measurably improve later work.\n\nThe current finding in [E-memory-topology.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/excursions/E-memory-topology.md:1) is therefore healthy. Subject leaves are often legitimate context; forcing them into shortcuts, or clique-projecting them, manufactures connectivity without knowledge.\n\nThe architecture I’d aim for is:\n\n```text\ntask/context\n    ↓\ncandidate generators\n  FTS · semantic search · explicit subjects · selected patterns\n    ↓\nadmissibility/warrant\n  domain · current/as-of · reviewed · provenance · witness\n    ↓\nbounded typed expansion\n  instantiates · supports · challenges · refines · supersedes · contrasts\n    ↓\nrank + diversify under one fixed budget\n    ↓\nmemories with explanation paths\n    ↓\nuse/outcome receipts → Librarian proposals and relation-weight evidence\n```\n\nThe content-match fix that has now landed in `50916c84` is actually a good instance of this architecture, not merely a bypass. Text search proposes; the reviewed graph still warrants; direct matches survive pattern arbitration. That discipline is explicit in [memory_recall.clj](/home/joe/code/futon3c/src/futon3c/peripheral/memory_recall.clj:486). I would crystallise it as:\n\n> Candidate generators may be plural; the evidential warrant remains singular.\n\nSeveral consequences follow.\n\nFirst, separate relation classes by function. A subject or session edge is usually context/filtering information, not a propagation channel. A pattern attachment says that a memory instantiates a reusable mechanism. `supports`, `challenges`, `supersedes`, `refines`, and `contrasts` can support controlled traversal. `distills` carries provenance. The same stored edge can appear in some derived projections and not others, without weakening the canonical record.\n\nSecond, never clique-project a hyperedge for operational diffusion. A handle touching \\(k\\) memories should remain an incidence relation, or use a degree-normalised hypergraph/random-walk operator. Clique expansion converts one assertion into \\(k(k-1)/2\\) apparent relations and is exactly the degree inflation Claude-9 detected. Typed incidence matrices \\(B_r\\), with one normalised transition operator per relation type, fit the intended \\(\\theta_r\\) evolution much better than one untyped adjacency matrix.\n\nThird, retire “global λ₂ exceeds 0.1” as a promotion gate. Preserve the original preregistered result as history, but version the meter. Meter v2 should report:\n\n- Both unnormalised and normalised spectra, explicitly labelled.\n- Degree distribution by node and relation type.\n- Component and isolate counts by domain and projection.\n- Hub concentration and pattern-size distribution.\n- Query-relative reachability: can each benchmark seed reach its relevant memories within one or two admissible hops?\n- Expansion precision: of the extra nodes reached through relation \\(r\\), how many were useful?\n- Per-edge-type ablations against held-out hit@k/MRR, latency, and eventual independently witnessed outcome.\n\nGlobal conductance then becomes a diagnostic, not the definition of utility. Intentional domain separation and specialist communities should not fail a health check.\n\nOn pattern minting, I would not mint more patterns merely to flatten the buckets. That would be the pattern-level equivalent of gaming λ₂. The existing P1b policy is substantially right: mint when repeated residual misses expose a reusable distinction that the current pattern vocabulary cannot express, with a falsifiable prediction and supporting/challenging memories ([M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md:836)).\n\nThe lifecycle should be:\n\n- Mint from a cluster of independently arising policy holes, not from corpus-count pressure.\n- Attach memories multi-label where appropriate; a memory need not belong to one exclusive bucket.\n- Retrieve from the top few pattern neighbourhoods plus direct content matches, merging and deduplicating under one fixed global budget.\n- Split a pattern when its triggering contexts or outcomes become internally heterogeneous.\n- Merge patterns when held-out behaviour cannot distinguish them.\n- Supersede rather than erase, retaining the bitemporal history.\n- Promote only after use and independently witnessed outcomes.\n\nThis removes the current bad incentive: finer patterns no longer create arbitrary “losers” merely because only one bucket may survive arbitration. It also preserves the original invariant that frequency and attachment mass are not value evidence.\n\nI would sequence the work like this:\n\n1. Land meter v2 and freeze a chronological query/relevance benchmark containing the known misses.\n2. Make multi-source, bounded candidate union the stable retrieval contract: direct matches plus top-\\(k\\) pattern neighbourhoods, with reason-bearing inclusion.\n3. Have the Librarian lift genuine narrative relations—supersedes, resolves, contrasts, refines—into reviewed typed edges. Do not invent bridge edges for connectivity.\n4. Measure edge-type lift on the frozen benchmark. Relations that add no retrieval value remain provenance/context relations rather than propagation channels.\n5. Only after the relation census and receipt volume are sufficient, evaluate typed \\(k\\)-step propagation against the hybrid lookup baseline.\n\nThe governing acceptance test would be:\n\n> A structural change is good only when it improves held-out retrieval or outcomes at the same read budget, while preserving domain, temporal, review, provenance, and witness invariants.\n\nThat gives the graph a way to earn its keep. It also permits the honest long-term result that much retrieval remains lexical or semantic, while the graph supplies admissibility, explanation, contradiction, lifecycle, and selective multi-hop transfer—the jobs for which graph structure is genuinely valuable.\n\nThe long-term strategy should be: **one authoritative evidence hypergraph, several purpose-built retrieval projections, and no attempt to make the authoritative graph globally “well connected.”**\n\nA good memory graph is not one with a large λ₂. It is one in which a query can reach useful, reviewed memories through meaningful typed paths within a bounded budget—and where those paths measurably improve later work.\n\nThe current finding in [E-memory-topology.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/excursions/E-memory-topology.md:1) is therefore healthy. Subject leaves are often legitimate context; forcing them into shortcuts, or clique-projecting them, manufactures connectivity without knowledge.\n\nThe architecture I’d aim for is:\n\n```text\ntask/context\n    ↓\ncandidate generators\n  FTS · semantic search · explicit subjects · selected patterns\n    ↓\nadmissibility/warrant\n  domain · current/as-of · reviewed · provenance · witness\n    ↓\nbounded typed expansion\n  instantiates · supports · challenges · refines · sup